Purpose: This article aims to synthesize and critically analyze the theoretical frameworks proposed to address the "problem of many hands" in modern public administration, where diffused responsibility complicates accountability. The study seeks to map the evolution of proposed solutions and identify the most promising path forward.&nbsp; 
<div>
 Design/methodology/approach: A systematic literature review was conducted following the PRISMA protocol. The review charts the conceptual dimensions and structural causes of the phenomenon, from classic bureaucracy to contemporary algorithmic governance, systematically identifying, selecting, and synthesizing relevant theoretical literature from 1980 to 2025.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Findings: The analysis reveals a critical paradigm shift in academic thought, moving away from retrospective blame assignment towards a prospective framework centered on "design responsibility". The findings suggest that an effective response lies not in identifying individual culprits, but in the deliberate design of resilient organizational structures, independent oversight mechanisms, and a culture of systemic learning.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Originality/value: The article's primary contribution is its cross-disciplinary synthesis and systematization of solution frameworks. By documenting the paradigm shift towards design responsibility, it offers an integrated and forward-looking framework for both the theory and practice of public administration, transforming an intractable ethical dilemma into a manageable, albeit demanding, problem of institutional design.
</div>
